  "answer_text": "Whether you are hoping for a positive or a negative, waiting for and interpreting pregnancy test results can bring up a mix of strong emotions. Because home urine tests rely on chemical reactions, understanding exactly what you are looking at is key to getting an accurate answer. \n\nAll home pregnancy tests work by detecting a hormone called **human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)**, which your body begins producing after a fertilized egg implants in the uterus. \n\n---\n\n### 1. How to Read Your Test Results\nThe most important step is to read the result **exactly within the time window** specified in your test's instructions (usually 3 to 5 minutes). Reading a test too early or too late can lead to an incorrect interpretation.\n\n*   **Positive Result:** \n    *   *Line Tests:* You will see two lines\u2014the control line (which proves the test worked) and the test line. **Even a very faint second line is considered a positive result**, as long as it appeared within the recommended testing window. \n    *   *Digital Tests:* The screen will clearly display words like \"Pregnant\" or \"Yes\".\n*   **Negative Result:** \n    *   *Line Tests:* Only one line (the control line) is visible. There is no second line at all in the test window.\n    *   *Digital Tests:* The screen will display \"Not Pregnant\" or \"No\".\n*   **Invalid Result:** \n    *   If **no control line** appears (even if a test line does), the test is broken or was not used correctly. You must discard it and try again with a new test.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Faint Line vs. Evaporation Line\nOne of the most common sources of confusion is seeing a barely visible second line. It is crucial to distinguish between a faint positive and an \"evaporation line\":\n\n| Feature | Faint Positive Line | Evaporation Line |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Color** | Has distinct color (pink or blue, depending on the test dye). | Colorless, gray, or looks like a faint shadow. |\n| **Timing** | Appears within the instruction window (usually 3\u20135 minutes). | Appears *after* the time window has passed and the test has dried. |\n| **Meaning** | Indicates early pregnancy (low hCG levels). | **Not** a positive result; it is just dried urine. |\n\n---\n\n### 3. What Can Cause an Inaccurate Result?\nWhile modern home tests are highly accurate (typically over 99% when used from the day of your missed period), errors can still happen:\n\n#### False Negatives (Testing negative when you are actually pregnant)\nThis is the most common testing error. It usually happens because:\n*   **Testing too early:** If you test before or right at the time of your missed period, your hCG levels might still be too low for the test to detect.\n*   **Diluted urine:** Drinking too much water right before a test can dilute your urine. For the most accurate result, **test first thing in the morning** when your urine is highly concentrated.\n*   **Incorrect usage:** Not saturating the test strip enough or checking the results too quickly.\n\n#### False Positives (Testing positive when you are not pregnant)\nTrue false positives are very rare because the test will only react if hCG is present. However, they can occur due to:\n*   A recent miscarriage, birth, or abortion (as hCG can take several weeks to leave your system).\n*   Certain fertility medications that contain hCG.\n*   A \"chemical pregnancy\" (a very early miscarriage that occurs shortly after implantation).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Next Steps & When to See a Doctor\n\n*   **If your test is positive:** A positive home test is highly reliable, but it **must be confirmed by a healthcare professional**. Schedule an appointment with an OB/GYN or a local clinic to arrange a clinical confirmation (such as a blood test or ultrasound) and discuss prenatal care.\n*   **If your test is negative but your period is still late:** Wait 3 to 4 days and test again, preferably with your first-morning urine. If you miss your period by more than a week and continue to test negative, contact a healthcare provider to explore other reasons your cycle might be delayed.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f **When to seek immediate emergency care:**\nRegardless of your pregnancy test result, if you are experiencing **severe one-sided pelvic or abdominal pain, heavy vaginal bleeding, shoulder pain, or dizziness/fainting**, you should seek immediate emergency medical evaluation. These can be signs of an **ectopic pregnancy** (a pregnancy growing outside the uterus), which is a life-threatening medical emergency.",
